  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 405 AM CDT Tue Jun 23 2026\n\nCurrently, closed mid level low centered over southern\nSaskatchewan with an embedded S/WV impulse rotating around the \nlow over north central North Dakota. An associated surface \ntrough extends from southern SK to south-southeast across \ncentral North Dakota. Showers continue to rotate around the S/WV\nand along the sfc trough, with a few thunderstorms over \nportions of south central ND into parts of the James River \nValley. Farther west, the sky is trending scattered to clear as\ndrier air moves in behind the wave. \n\nMorning showers and storms will continue to push east with the\nsfc trough, with some showers continuing across the north this \nmorning with cyclonic flow aloft. Mid level low develops slowly \neast-southeast along the International Border region through the\nday, and is expected to result in diurnally driven showers and \na few thunderstorms over my north this afternoon and evening. \nSPC continues with the Marginal outlook for the James River \nValley, though with the sfc trough east of there and all CAMs \nshowing afternoon convection firing up along to east of the Red \nRiver Valley, I've opted not to include any mention in the HWO \nor other products at this time. \n\nWe remain in northwest flow aloft Wednesday, with another round\nof diurnally driven convection forecast. S/WV mid level ridge\napproaches and builds into the Northern Plains for Thursday,\nresulting in mainly dry weather during the day. This is then\nfollowed by another S/WV trough bringing showers and\nthunderstorm chances in from the west Thu night. \n\nLarge upper level low slides southeast along the western \nCanadian coast on Thursday, then into the Pacific Northwest on\nFriday, resulting in an active southwest flow aloft developing\nfor the Northern Plains region this coming weekend. NBM remains\nfairly high with precip chances, with models hinting at an\nelevated risk for strong to severe storms this weekend as well. \nForecast temperatures through Friday remain near to a little \nbelow seasonable normals, then closer to normal this coming \nweekend. Normal for late June are in the upper 70s/low 80s for \ndaytime highs and mid 50s for morning lows.",
